{"type":"user","uuid":"1c2b0f81-caaa-4e5f-83a1-0ec77fa3fdc7","session_id":"9cd663a4-03ec-40d7-9407-ed0797a59eb3","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_d184d4a954934e7cb16d63ee","is_error":false,"content":"package com.trianguloy.urlchecker.modules.list;\n\nimport android.net.Uri;\nimport android.net.UrlQuerySanitizer;\nimport android.view.View;\nimport android.widget.Button;\nimport android.widget.LinearLayout;\nimport android.widget.TextView;\n\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.R;\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.activities.ModulesActivity;\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.dialogs.MainDialog;\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.modules.AModuleConfig;\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.modules.AModuleData;\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.modules.AModuleDialog;\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.modules.DescriptionConfig;\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.url.UrlData;\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.utilities.methods.AndroidUtils;\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.utilities.methods.Inflater;\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.utilities.methods.JavaUtils;\nimport com.trianguloy.urlchecker.utilities.methods.JavaUtils.Supplier;\n\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\n/** This module shows all parts of the url decoded */\npublic class UriPartsModule extends AModuleData {\n\n    @Override\n    public String getId() {\n        return \"uriparts\";\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int getName() {\n        return R.string.mParts_name;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public AModuleDialog getDialog(MainDialog cntx) {\n        return new UriPartsDialog(cntx);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public AModuleConfig getConfig(ModulesActivity cntx) {\n        return new DescriptionConfig(R.string.mParts_desc);\n    }\n}\n\nclass UriPartsDialog extends AModuleDialog {\n\n    private LinearLayout box;\n    private final List<String> expandedGroups = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    public UriPartsDialog(MainDialog dialog) {\n        super(dialog);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int getLayoutId() {\n        return R.layout.dialog_parts;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onInitialize(View views) {\n        box = views.findViewById(R.id.box);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onDisplayUrl(UrlData urlData) {\n        // clear\n        box.removeAllViews();\n\n        // parse\n        var uri = Uri.parse(urlData.url);\n        var urlQuerySanitizer = new UrlQuerySanitizer();\n        // the default parseUrl doesn't remove the fragment\n        if (uri.getQuery() != null) {\n            urlQuerySanitizer.setAllowUnregisteredParamaters(true);\n            urlQuerySanitizer.setUnregisteredParameterValueSanitizer(v -> v);\n            urlQuerySanitizer.parseQuery(uri.getQuery()\n                    // this will fix issues with the parser decoding twice\n                    .replace(\"%\", \"%25\"));\n        }\n\n        // domain elements\n        if (uri.getAuthority() != null || uri.getScheme() != null) {\n            var domain = addGroup(\"Domain\", -1, null);\n            addPart(\"scheme\", uri.getScheme(), domain, null);\n            addPart(\"user info\", uri.getUserInfo(), domain, null);\n            addPart(\"host\", uri.getHost(), domain, null);\n            addPart(\"port\", uri.getPort() != -1 ? String.valueOf(uri.getPort()) : null, domain, null);\n        }\n\n        // paths\n        var pathSegments = uri.getPathSegments();\n        if (!pathSegments.isEmpty()) {\n            var paths = addGroup(\"Paths\", pathSegments.size(), uri.buildUpon().path(null));\n            for (var i = 0; i < pathSegments.size(); i++) {\n                int removeI = i;\n                // append the path\n                addPart(\"/\", pathSegments.get(i), paths, () -> {\n                    // generate the same url without this path\n                    var builder = uri.buildUpon();\n                    builder.path(null);\n                    for (int newI = 0; newI < pathSegments.size(); newI++) {\n                        if (newI != removeI) builder.appendPath(pathSegments.get(newI));\n                    }\n                    return builder.build().toString();\n                });\n            }\n        }\n\n        // query parameters\n        var parameters = urlQuerySanitizer.getParameterList();\n        if (!parameters.isEmpty()) {\n            var queries = addGroup(\"Parameters\", parameters.size(), uri.buildUpon().query(null));\n            for (var i = 0; i < parameters.size(); i++) {\n                int removeI = i;\n                // append the parameter\n                addPart(parameters.get(i).mParameter, parameters.get(i).mValue, queries, () -> {\n                    // generate same url but without this parameter\n                    var builder = uri.buildUpon();\n                    builder.query(null);\n                    for (var newI = 0; newI < parameters.size(); newI++) {\n                        if (newI != removeI) builder.appendQueryParameter(parameters.get(newI).mParameter, parameters.get(newI).mValue);\n                    }\n                    return builder.build().toString();\n                });\n            }\n        }\n\n        // fragment\n        if (uri.getFragment() != null) {\n            var fragment = addGroup(\"Fragment\", -1, uri.buildUpon().fragment(null));\n            addPart(\"#\", uri.getFragment(), fragment, null);\n        }\n\n        setVisibility(box.getChildCount() > 0);\n    }\n\n    /** Adds a collapsible group */\n    private LinearLayout addGroup(String name, int size, Uri.Builder onDelete) {\n        var title = Inflater.inflate(R.layout.uri_part, box);\n        title.findViewById(R.id.key).setVisibility(View.GONE);\n\n        var name_view = title.<TextView>findViewById(R.id.value);\n        name_view.setText(name + (size <= -1 ? \"\" : \" (\" + size + \")\"));\n        AndroidUtils.setAsClickable(name_view);\n\n        var delete_view = title.<Button>findViewById(R.id.delete);\n        if (onDelete == null) delete_view.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n        else delete_view.setOnClickListener(v -> setUrl(onDelete.build().toString()));\n\n        var group = Inflater.<LinearLayout>inflate(R.layout.dialog_parts, box);\n        group.setVisibility(expandedGroups.contains(name) ? View.VISIBLE : View.GONE);\n        AndroidUtils.toggleableListener(\n                title,\n                v -> JavaUtils.toggleContains(expandedGroups, name),\n                v -> {\n                    group.setVisibility(expandedGroups.contains(name) ? View.VISIBLE : View.GONE);\n                    name_view.setCompoundDrawablesRelativeWithIntrinsicBounds(expandedGroups.contains(name) ? R.drawable.arrow_down : R.drawable.arrow_right, 0, 0, 0);\n                }\n        );\n\n        return group;\n    }\n\n    /** Adds a part */\n    private void addPart(String name, String value, LinearLayout container, Supplier<String> onDelete) {\n        if (value == null) return;\n        // create row\n        var part = Inflater.inflate(R.layout.uri_part, container);\n\n        // configure key\n        var key_view = part.<TextView>findViewById(R.id.key);\n        key_view.setText(name.isEmpty() ? getActivity().getString(R.string.mParts_empty) : name);\n        if (!name.isEmpty()) key_view.setOnLongClickListener(longTapToCopy);\n\n        // configure value\n        var value_view = part.<TextView>findViewById(R.id.value);\n        value_view.setText(value);\n        AndroidUtils.setAsClickable(value_view);\n        value_view.setOnClickListener(v -> setUrl(value));\n        value_view.setOnLongClickListener(longTapToCopy);\n\n        // configure delete\n        var delete_view = part.<Button>findViewById(R.id.delete);\n        if (onDelete != null) {\n            delete_view.setOnClickListener(v -> setUrl(onDelete.get()));\n        } else {\n            delete_view.setVisibility(View.GONE);\n        }\n    }\n\n    /** OnLongClickListener to copy a part (textview text) to the clipboard */\n    private final View.OnLongClickListener longTapToCopy = v -> {\n        AndroidUtils.copyToClipboard(getActivity(), R.string.mParts_copy, ((TextView) v).getText().toString());\n        return true;\n    };\n\n}\n"}]}}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"14d3060d-d77b-491f-b4af-edb4c7a03c97","session_id":"9cd663a4-03ec-40d7-9407-ed0797a59eb3","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"14d3060d-d77b-491f-b4af-edb4c7a03c97","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see the bug. The key issue is in the `onDisplayUrl` method, specifically in how `UrlQuerySanitizer` parses the query string:\n\n```java\nif (uri.getQuery() != null) {\n    urlQuerySanitizer.setAllowUnregisteredParamaters(true);\n    urlQuerySanitizer.setUnregisteredParameterValueSanitizer(v -> v);\n    urlQuerySanitizer.parseQuery(uri.getQuery()\n            // this will fix issues with the parser decoding twice\n            .replace(\"%\", \"%25\"));\n}\n```\n\nThe problem is that `UrlQuerySanitizer.parseQuery()` splits the query string by `&` first, then by `=`. But when it splits by `&`, it's splitting on the raw (not yet decoded) string. The code is replacing `%` with `%25` before parsing, which prevents URL decoding.\n\nFor the URL `https://example.com/?q=a%26b%3Dc`:\n- `uri.getQuery()` returns `q=a%26b%3Dc` (the raw query string)\n- After `.replace(\"%\", \"%25\")`, it becomes `q=a%2526b%253Dc`\n- `UrlQuerySanitizer.parseQuery()` then splits by `&` and `=`\n- But the issue is that `UrlQuerySanitizer` internally decodes the values, so `%26` becomes `&` during parsing\n\nWait, let me re-read this. The comment says \"this will fix issues with the parser decoding twice\", and they replace `%` with `%25` to prevent decoding. But the bug description says:\n\nInput: `https://example.com/?q=a%26b%3Dc`\nExpected: `q --> a&b=c` (the value is URL-decoded)\n\n\nActual: `q --> a`, `b --> c` (split incorrectly)\n\nSo the bug is that the values ARE being decoded (which is correct behavior), but the splitting on `&` happens BEFORE decoding. The `UrlQuerySanitizer` first splits the query string by `&` to identify parameter boundaries, then decodes the individual values. So with `q=a%26b%3Dc`, it splits on the literal `&` characters first, treating `q=a` and `b` as separate parameters, then decodes each one. That's why `q` gets the value `a` instead of `a&b=c`. `parseQuery(\"q=a%26b%3Dc\")`:\n1. Split by `&`: `[\"q=a%26b%3Dc\"]` - only one element since there's no literal `&`\n2. Then split by `=`: `[\"q\", \"a%26b%3Dc\"]`\n3. Decode the value: `a&b=c`\n\nSo `q` should map to `a&b=c`, which matches the expected behavior. The issue might be that `uri.getQuery()` from Android's `Uri.parse()` is already decoding the query string before it reaches `parseQuery()`, causing the `%26` to become `&` prematurely.\n\nIf the query string is pre-decoded to `q=a&b=c`, then splitting on `&` would incorrectly produce `[\"q=a\", \"b=c\"]` instead of the single parameter. The code attempts to prevent this by replacing `%` with `%25` before parsing, so `%26` becomes `%2526`, but the order of operations in `UrlQuerySanitizer.parseQuery()` might be causing the decoding to happen at the wrong stage. The `.replace(\"%\", \"%25\")` transform prevents double decoding but leaves the query string unsplit on literal `&` characters. The real issue is that Android's `Uri.getQuery()` might already be URL-decoding the query string before it reaches `parseQuery()`, which would cause the split-on-`&` logic to break if encoded delimiters were decoded prematurely. Let me trace through `UrlQuerySanitizer.parseQuery()` to see exactly where the splitting and decoding happen. `&` in the raw query string. When I trace through with the example URL, if `uri.getQuery()` returns the raw string `q=a%26b%3Dc`, the replace operation would convert it to `q=a%2526b%253Dc` to prevent double decoding. But if the bug is occurring, it suggests `uri.getQuery()` is already returning the decoded version `q=a&b=c` instead of the raw encoded form, which would cause the replace to have no effect and the parser to incorrectly split on the literal `&`.\n\nThe core issue is that `UrlQuerySanitizer` expects a raw query string with percent-encoded delimiters, but Android's `Uri` class decodes the query during parsing. The `.replace(\"%\", \"%25\")` workaround was meant to prevent double-decoding after the sanitizer processes it, but it doesn't solve the fundamental mismatch where the encoded `&` has already been decoded before the sanitizer even sees the string.\n\nLooking at the actual bug behavior, `UrlQuerySanitizer.parseQuery()` is splitting on literal `&` characters, producing separate parameters instead of keeping them together. The issue is that `%26` (the percent-encoded `&`) is being decoded too early in the process, so the sanitizer encounters actual `&` delimiters rather than their encoded forms.\n\nThe `.replace(\"%\", \"%25\")` approach was meant to escape special characters before parsing, but this doesn't solve the core problem—it just shifts what gets decoded. I need to look at how `UrlQuerySanitizer` actually handles the parsing to understand why the encoded ampersand is being treated as a delimiter. I need to understand how `UrlQuerySanitizer` actually processes the query string. The core issue is that Android's `Uri.getQuery()` returns a decoded string, which means any encoded `&` characters have already been converted to literal `&`, causing `UrlQuerySanitizer` to incorrectly split parameters at those points instead of treating them as part of the value. The `.replace(\"%\", \"%25\")` workaround doesn't solve this because it can't recover what was already decoded. I need to reconsider how `parseQuery()` actually handles the input string. The method splits on `&` first to separate parameters, then on `=` to split keys from values, but crucially, it doesn't perform URL decoding on either the parameter names or values—those get handled separately through the sanitizers. So if `getQuery()` is returning a raw query string like `q=a%26b%3Dc`, the sanitizer would parse it as a single parameter with key `q` and encoded value `a%26b%3Dc`.\n\nThe problem emerges if `getQuery()` returns the decoded version `q=a&b=c` instead—then splitting on `&` produces two separate parameters: `q=a` and `b=c`, which is incorrect. This suggests `getQuery()` is returning decoded output, possibly because the URI itself contains decoded `&` characters rather than their percent-encoded equivalents. The `Uri.parse()` method might be decoding the query string during parsing, which would explain why the bug report shows the actual behavior differs from what I'd expect.\n\nLooking at the code more carefully, I see a workaround that replaces `%` with `%25` before parsing. This prevents double-decoding when the sanitizer's internal mechanisms process the string, but it doesn't solve the core issue of how `getQuery()` handles the raw query string in the first place.\n\nThe real problem is that I'm fighting Android's URI parsing behavior. I should either extract the raw query string directly from the URL or use `Uri.getQueryParameterNames()` and `getQueryParameter()` to handle the decoding consistently rather than trying to escape it beforehand. I'm realizing the core issue: `UrlQuerySanitizer` splits on unescaped `&` and `=`, but Android's `Uri.getQuery()` already decodes percent-encoded characters, so `%26` becomes `&` and breaks the parsing. The solution is to extract the raw query string directly from the URL instead of relying on Android's decoded version, preserving the original encoding until we're ready to process it properly.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
