{"type":"assistant","uuid":"06717113-7109-433b-8235-b9779fb8cfc1","session_id":"9cd663a4-03ec-40d7-9407-ed0797a59eb3","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"06717113-7109-433b-8235-b9779fb8cfc1","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The bug is fixed. Here's a summary:\n\n## Bug Analysis\n\n**Root Cause:** In `UriPartsModule.java`, the code used `uri.getQuery()` to extract the query string before passing it to `UrlQuerySanitizer.parseQuery()`. Android's `Uri.getQuery()` **URL-decodes** the query string, converting `%26` → `&` and `%3D` → `=`. \n\n`UrlQuerySanitizer.parseQuery()` splits on literal `&` first (parameter delimiter), then on `=` (key-value delimiter), and **then** decodes values. When the query string was pre-decoded by `Uri.getQuery()`, the `%26` had already become a literal `&`, causing `UrlQuerySanitizer` to incorrectly split `q=a&b=c` into two parameters (`q=a`, `b=c`) instead of one (`q=a&b=c`).\n\nThe previous workaround of `.replace(\"%\", \"%25\")` tried to prevent double-decoding but couldn't fix the fundamental problem — the `&` was already decoded by the time `UrlQuerySanitizer` saw it.\n\n## Fix\n\nExtract the raw query string directly from `urlData.url` by parsing the `?` and `#` positions, bypassing Android's `Uri.getQuery()` entirely. This preserves `%26` as literal `%26` when passed to `UrlQuerySanitizer.parseQuery()`, which correctly splits on the `&` delimiter and then decodes `%26` → `&` in the value.\n\n**Before:** `q --> a`, `b --> c` (split at decoded `&`)\n**After:** `q --> a&b=c` (correctly parsed as one parameter with decoded value)"}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":42587,"output_tokens":389,"cache_read_input_tokens":41024,"total_tokens":42976}}}
